WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.160
Container isolation is assembled from several controls rather than provided by one wall.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:13.700
Linux namespaces give processes separate views of resources such as process identifiers, mounts, networking, host names, and users.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.560
The exact namespaces enabled and their configuration define part of the boundary.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:25.500
Control groups, or cgroups, account for and limit resources such as CPU, memory, and process counts.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:33.400
They support availability and workload governance, but resource limits alone do not authorize access or create complete security isolation.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:37.460
Linux capabilities divide powerful system privileges into smaller units.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.520
Removing capabilities reduces what a process can ask the kernel to do.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:48.300
Granting broad or unnecessary capabilities weakens the least-privilege assumption even when the workload still runs inside namespaces.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:52.840
Filesystem isolation normally presents a container-specific root filesystem.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:01:00.980
Image layers, writable layers, volumes, device access, and host mounts determine what data crosses that boundary.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:07.200
A mount can intentionally share information while also expanding the assets exposed to the workload.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:13.100
The container runtime creates and manages these controls and communicates with the host kernel.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:19.800
Runtime configuration, interfaces, versions, and permissions are therefore part of the trust model.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:24.580
A container remains dependent on the security and correctness of the shared kernel.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:32.160
Threat modeling should record each isolation mechanism, the privileges and mounts granted, and the expected host interfaces.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:37.100
Defense in depth matters because no single control should carry the entire containment claim.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:43.960
Workload restrictions, runtime hardening, node security, orchestration policy, and monitoring reinforce one another.
